<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mozarabs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mozarab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mozarabs were Christians who lived under Muslim rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, adopting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabic-language">Arabic language</a> and culture while keeping their faith. Many migrated north to the Christian kingdoms from the 9th century, where they helped resettle frontier lands in León, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Portugal. They kept a distinctive liturgy, the Mozarabic rite, and their name also covers the Romance dialects spoken in al-Andalus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mozarabs.jpg" alt="Mozarabs" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fogar Mozárabe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arcos_de_herradura_moz%C3%A1rabe_de_Santiago_de_Pe%C3%B1alba.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
